## 4. Motion & Theme

### [CXC-ICV-MOTION] Motion Inventory — Scene-Graph Animation, Reduced-Motion Gated

- definition: the pre-vis motion is CSS `@keyframes`; the console is a Skia SCENE, so every animation is scene-graph animation — a clocked widget implements `on_clock_tick(now: float) -> bool` (the same deterministic clock the Lottie widget uses via `register_clocked` in the RenderContext, [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE] [CXC-LOT-DETERMINISM]) and computes its animated value from `now` (returning True while animating so the runtime redraws). Continuous loops (breathe, blink, beat, pulse) are periodic functions of `now`; they do NOT use the `Animator` (that is for one-shot eased property transitions). The inventory (timing + easing lifted from the pre-vis `@keyframes`):
  - `beat` (header live-dot, live status): period 2.6s, ease-in-out, opacity 1.0 → 0.35 → 1.0.
  - `breathe` (avatar halo): period 5.5s, ease-in-out, `scale` 1.0 → 1.06 and opacity 0.85 → 1.0 (about the halo center).
  - `blink` (avatar eyes): period 6.4s, a STEP function — eyes at full height ~96% of the cycle, `scaleY` collapses to ~0.12 for a ~60ms flick (the pre-vis `5.5%–6.5%` of the period).
  - `hotpulse` (gauge lead arc): period 2.4s, ease-in-out, opacity 1.0 → 0.45 — ACTIVE ONLY in the 90 (crit) zone.
  - `runpulse` (subagent running dot): period 1.6s, ease-in-out, opacity 1.0 → 0.35.
- **reduced-motion** (the one motion system the engine LACKS today — no `reduced_motion` exists in `render/engine/`): introduce a SCENE-level reduced-motion flag (a `design.reduced_motion: true` key in the `.compose.yaml`, plumbed onto the RenderContext, default false; the flag is the scene equivalent of the CSS `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`). Every clocked widget CONSULTS the flag in `on_clock_tick`: when set, it renders the REST frame (breathe/beat/pulse at opacity 1.0/scale 1.0; eyes open) and returns False (no per-frame redraw). This mirrors exactly what the pre-vis gates: `.avatar .halo, .avatar .eyes, .live i, .subs .st.run, .gauge .lead` all `animation:none`. Motion is ambient and calm by design — nothing flashes, nothing demands attention except, deliberately, the gauge hot-pulse at 90.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CONSOLE-VISUAL]

### [CXC-ICV-THEME] Theming — a Token Swap, Not a CSS Cascade

- definition: the pre-vis themes via CSS custom-property overrides (`:root[data-theme]` + `@media prefers-color-scheme`); the console has no cascade — theme is a TOKEN SWAP. The design-system module ([CXC-ICV-TOKENS]) exposes TWO token tables (`DARK`, `LIGHT`) and a single `active` selector; the scene's `design.theme: dark|light` (already parsed, `parser.py` line 82/373/685) picks the table at mount, and every widget reads `CONSOLE.active.<token>`. Switching theme = re-point `active` + `request_redraw()` — no widget code changes, no per-widget theme branches. DARK is primary (the identity); LIGHT is the legible daylight variant, fully specified in [CXC-ICV-TOKENS]. The header theme button ([CXC-ICV-CHROME]) toggles `active` and redraws.
- invariant: NO widget hardcodes a hex or a theme test. A widget that needs a color asks the token by ROLE (`accent`, `crit_dim`, `panel_2`); the role resolves per active table. This is what makes both themes correct from ONE implementation and keeps the two palettes in sync with a single edit.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CONSOLE-VISUAL]

